{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<h1>Clean up</h1>
<p class="hint">Tables still in <code>{{database}}</code> that this software no longer uses. They are
  left behind on purpose: upgrading only ever <em>adds</em> to a database, so a version that removes
  a feature cannot take somebody's data with it, and an operator who upgrades and then rolls back
  still has everything. The cost is what is listed here.</p>

{{^any}}<p class="done">Nothing left over. Every table in this database is one the software still
  uses.</p>{{/any}}

{{#any}}
<p class="count">{{count}} table(s), holding {{rowsHeld}} row(s)</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>table</th><th>rows</th><th></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{{#tables}}<tr>
    <td class="mono">{{name}}</td>
    <td class="dim mono">{{rows}}</td>
    <td class="actions">
      <form class="inline" method="post" action="{{action}}" onsubmit="return confirm('Drop {{name}} and everything in it? This cannot be undone.')">
        <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{csrf}}">
        <input type="hidden" name="action" value="drop">
        <input type="hidden" name="table" value="{{name}}">
        <button type="submit">drop</button>
      </form>
    </td>
  </tr>{{/tables}}</tbody>
</table>
<p class="problem"><strong>Dropping a table deletes what is in it, and there is no undo on this
  screen.</strong> A table with rows in it is somebody's discussion board, or their calendar, or
  their address book, from before those features were removed. If any of it might still be wanted,
  take a copy of the database directory first &mdash; that is the whole backup, and it is a file
  copy.</p>
<p class="hint">One at a time and no &lsquo;drop them all&rsquo;, because reading each name is the
  point. A table this software still uses can never appear here, whatever a form is made to
  say.</p>
{{/any}}
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
